WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.870
Film Courage: Did you see the movie THANK
YOU FOR SMOKING?

00:00:01.870 --> 00:00:06.689
So Rob Lowe’s character [Jeff Megall, entertainment
executive] that’s what I envision is this

00:00:06.689 --> 00:00:07.770
person.

00:00:07.770 --> 00:00:13.940
Daniel Stamm: You know that in one minute
it doesn’t take long for the first meeting

00:00:13.940 --> 00:00:19.920
because there are certain buzzwords that executives
I guess in their 20-minute story training

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:25.680
get told that if someone uses it that early
on in a conversation we are [BLEEP!].

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:27.990
Film Courage: I’m curious what those buzzwords
are?

00:00:27.990 --> 00:00:31.340
Daniel Stamm, Filmmaker: You know one big
thing is “elevated genre, we want this to

00:00:31.340 --> 00:00:33.020
be elevated.”

00:00:33.020 --> 00:00:34.020
Sure?

00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:36.500
What does that mean?

00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:39.720
They want smart genre, the want blah, blah,
blah, blah.

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:42.030
Sure, but that doesn’t help us with the
development.

00:00:42.030 --> 00:00:44.510
“Organic, the characters have to be organic.”

00:00:44.510 --> 00:00:46.300
“It has to be character driven.”

00:00:46.300 --> 00:00:49.940
It’s these words and they are all true but
there are clichés.

00:00:49.940 --> 00:00:55.940
They came out of good observation so if someone’s
only contribution during the first meeting

00:00:55.940 --> 00:01:00.500
is to ask for a character-driven elevated
horror story, I’m in trouble because it’s

00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:04.750
not going to be much actual help coming from
that side.

00:01:04.750 --> 00:01:11.140
Film Courage: With these catch phrases I hear
a lot of Millennials use the term “That’s

00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:12.470
so meta!”

00:01:12.470 --> 00:01:15.610
Or “It’s so epic.”

00:01:15.610 --> 00:01:16.900
So those kind of buzzwords.

00:01:16.900 --> 00:01:18.870
I’m still trying to wrap my head around
it.

00:01:18.870 --> 00:01:24.230
I know what the word means but not in the
context of daily life, how is some experience

00:01:24.230 --> 00:01:25.780
“so meta?”

00:01:25.780 --> 00:01:26.780
I don’t know?

00:01:26.780 --> 00:01:29.580
I’m showing my age with this statement.

00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:35.480
Daniel: Right, you know that when you actually
ask someone to define that, there is “Well

00:01:35.480 --> 00:01:37.350
you know.”

00:01:37.350 --> 00:01:38.350
“No I don’t know.

00:01:38.350 --> 00:01:43.020
What does that…how do you mean organic?”

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:48.690
And then you also get answers to certain buzzphrases
to questions like when you go “I think that

00:01:48.690 --> 00:01:50.170
will be confusing.”

00:01:50.170 --> 00:01:54.280
They will go “Oh, the audience today is
smarter than that.”

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:58.950
You can say that to every story problem “the
audience today is smarter than that.”

00:01:58.950 --> 00:02:01.390
That is not an approach.

00:02:01.390 --> 00:02:07.979
So when you get a series of those kinds of
things you kind of know that…yeah.

